00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.150 align:start position:0%
 
you  welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wiltshire host and our guest is  Louisa Lombard an assistant professor of  anthropology at Yale University she is a  cultural anthropologist who studies  African borderland areas where the state  is largely absent and a range of actors  govern her research locales primarily  the remote and little populated eastern  reaches of the Central African Republic  are further marked by violent histories  that continue into the present professor  lumbars main fieldwork interlocutors are  among the region's men in arms such as  anti-poaching guards and rebels today we  talk with Professor Lombard about her  recently published paper on violence  popular punishment and the war in the  Central African Republic welcome  professor Lombard thanks it's great to  be here so let's begin with an overview  of your paper tell us about it so  there's been a war on going in the  Central African Republic for the past  couple of years and a lot of people both  in the Central African Republic and  Beyond have been surprised by how  quickly the violence spread and came to  involve so much of the population a  range of different explanations have  been brought for us as to how this  happened politicians for instance the  president of the country has argued that  this is a case of manipulation that  politico-military entrepreneurs are  manipulating people into fighting into  perpetuating the war and in this paper  we argue that although those factors are  indeed quite important they miss  something really important as well and  that is the important place of violent  popular punishment in the country even  prior to the war and we argue that you  know war war is not a complete break  from the past it doesn't reflect a total  new situation it reflects rather the  continuation the transformation of  existing social practices so we try  to explain a little bit about how this  context of popular punishment developed  in the Central African Republic both  through the colonial period into the  independent era and as the country has  really seen its economic situation be  plummet and be eviscerated and a  widespread feeling of insecurity has  come to dominate people's lives and in  that context of insecurity and of even a  kind of pervasive enemy by which I mean  the situation in which people feel like  norms existing norms no longer really no  longer really help them understand their  lives and no longer really serve to  control the kinds of threats that they  feel like they face in that kind of  context popular punishment became quite  widespread and often entailed quite a  bit of violence as well okay what do you  mean by popular punishment that's an  excellent question so when we and I  should say that this is an article that  I co-authored with a central african  colleague sylvan batanga kinsey so when  we're using this term popular punishment  we're referring to things that other  scholars have referred to as popular  justice kind of vigilantism people for  instance when there's a suspected thief  who is apprehended and that thief is  beaten by a crowd to try to you know  show him that this is not something that  he should be doing and the reason that  we use the term popular punishment is  because we think that it's more  analytically precise the term justice if  we're referring to popular justice it  implies that this is something that  people feel is you know the ideal way  that a problem should be resolved and  what we see instead is not people are  very ambivalent about this popular  punishment they see it as necessary  given the grave threats that they face  but they see it as very very far from  ideal and we wanted to capture that that  people are talking about the need to  punish those the the people who are  bringing the threats into their lives  but that that is not exactly the same  thing as justice right so rather than  the police handling the problem people  are dealing with the situation  themselves yes and sometimes the police  sometimes people will hire individual  members of the police or the gendarmerie  to come and help them in this regard but  they're doing that the the the police  officers are then acting in there a kind  of private capacity and often will  participate in the violence themselves  and why would that happen versus the  police having a role in arresting the  thief and bringing them to jail well the  police you know our perennial II  under-resourced they don't really have  the capacity to deal with these kinds of  situations themselves and for that  matter they're often participating in  these kinds of violent punishments  themselves and they see it as necessary  also I mean one of our during one of our  interviews were with a gendarme who in  the middle of the night I noticed that  somebody was trying to break into his  house so he went and found the person  and began beating the person the  commotion drew other people from his  neighborhood who came in and started  beating the person as well they ended up  beating the man to within just an inch  of his life and at that point the  gendarme thought well maybe this wasn't  the right way to deal with this and they  managed to stop the beating and bring  the man to the hospital but he died  shortly thereafter and that John darme  said look the Jean de Marie is corrupt  everybody is corrupt and so we have to  take matters into our own hands right  you would think though that with that  level of punishment no one no crimes  would be committed but one would hope  but that's always the you know I mean we  see this elsewhere as well that the the  deterrent factor that is hoped for in  punishment for crimes does not always  work in the ways that we were would like  I mean of course capital punishment is a  perfect example of that you know what  led you to do this kind of work how did  you find yourself in central Africa  well I that's a long story at this point  but while I was an undergraduate it was  close to when I was graduating and I  went to a talk by somebody about his  research in the Central African Republic  and I but here I am and I've been  studying Africa for all this time and  here's a country that I've barely even  heard of how is this possible I want to  know more and so I got to know that man  and ended up working for him he was  became the managing director of a  research institute called the small arms  survey and I went back to went to the  country a number of times working for  them doing research on the social  impacts of having guns pretty widespread  in the society now that was before the  war broke out or just about during yes  so I should clarify a little bit there  there's been a lot of conflict in the  Central African Republic over the last  mmm 20 years or so I it's intermittent  it'll happen in different parts of the  country at different times it's only in  the last couple of years that we've seen  a real qualitative shift and it's become  a situation that I would describe as war  and who is the war between I mean is it  more political base more religious based  of combination so that's an excellent  question if you read newspaper articles  about what's going on in the Central  African Republic they'll often say that  there are two main groups that there is  the seleka rebel alliance and that  there's the anti-balaka rebel group the  problem with that way of thinking about  what's going on in the country is that  having these the these two names for the  different the opposing sides masks the  fact that what we have here is not two  groups with a kind of organized  hierarchy and structures of command and  objectives and all of that instead we  have two very diffuse processes of  violent mobilization that are in many  cases happening on a kind of village  basis and so it's a much more diffuse  and widespread process of mobilization  than a lot of the more journalistic  accounts might lead you to believe  now this is one of the reasons why it's  been so hard to draw the fighting to an  end it's because there aren't two people  you know one person on each side who you  can have sit down together and talk  through a peace process and then they  tell their subordinates and their  subordinates tell their subordinates now  it's time to stop instead again this is  a much more non centralized kind of war  in which people are fighting over things  that in many cases are reflect sort of  long-standing localized tensions more  than they do any kind of grand political  objective and how how large of an area  are we talking about and how many people  are involved well it's that's a  difficult question for one thing just  because it's it's very difficult to  obtain information about what's going on  in areas outside of the capital at this  point things have been calming down the  UN just deployed a peacekeeping force to  the Central African Republic just two  days ago so there so the violence is at  a lower point at this point but it has  involved I would say I you know the  southwestern part of the country the  northwestern part of the country the  northeastern part of the country really  the only area that has been largely  spared as the southeastern corner of the  country which is just very hard to get  to and has its own dynamics of conflict  it's one of the areas where the Lord's  Resistance Army is believed to be  hanging out so okay you know I'm curious  about you know how the methodology to  write the article you know how did you  do it I mean it seems like it would be  somewhat difficult to get all of the  information you need based on what  you've said so how did you go about  writing it well how did we go about  writing it or how did we go about doing  the research research so I'm an  anthropologist so I do ethnographic  field research and that involves a lot  of you know deep hanging out getting to  know people spending time with them  observing participating in their lives  and then also doing interviews and  trying to find out  we're what the sources of social tension  are and this article is really the  product of a very long engagement with  the Central African Republic howland so  I've been going there since 2003 so over  10 years at this point and I wanted to  you know I felt like a lot of the people  who were trying to explain what was  going on in the in the war over the last  two years we're acting as if prior to  the outbreak of war in 2012 there had  been a kind of peaceful baseline and so  I wanted to draw attention to the fact  that I had observed a lot of violence  over the time over my decade of of  getting to know people in the country  and that this is something that I think  is troubling and needs to be addressed  in spending so much time there were you  ever fearful for your own life as a  woman and as a white woman you know  that's a question that I get asked a lot  the short answer is no I was not the  longer answer is that I through the  years I've built up a network of people  I trust and I ask them I ask them what  is it's safe for me to do what I should  avoid doing what's going to arouse  suspicions and I listen to them and I  follow their advice and so if somebody  who you know whose hospitality I would  be relying on says no don't come don't  come here right now you know don't come  to this town then I listen to them and I  don't go there and I'll go somewhere  else because I'm really interested in  trying to better understand dynamics of  conflict how they work how it happens  all of these kinds of things but I'm not  interested in getting myself into  situations that I can't get myself out  of and i think that my method you know  the ethnographic method in some senses  is helpful in this regard because i know  that in order to do effective research i  have to be able to rely on trust from my  interlocutors and if i'm going into a  situation where tensions are just too  high then i'm not going to be able to do  that anyway so better to you know better  to save  energies for places where I can do my  research more effectively okay let's  talk about some of your findings in your  paper tell us about you know some of the  conclusions that you come to so I in the  conclusion of the paper I we argue that  again this is coming out of my  experience both doing research in the  country and then also providing  briefings and other kinds of things for  different members of international  agencies that are trying to stop the  violence or and build peace in the  country you know and those types of  organizations they tend to focus on  institutions they tend to focus on the  formal state structures which in the  Central African Republic are extremely  weak so that's an important goal in  certain respects but at the same time I  think that in order to prevent real  outbreaks of violence in the country in  the future we need to pay greater  attention also to the place of violence  and even vengeance and popular  punishment in central Africans sort of  conceptions of how to deal with threats  that they face in their midst because  this situation a very pervasive  insecurity that is probably not going to  end anytime soon so there needs to be  some kind of reckoning as to you know  reckoning for central Africans among  central Africans as to the place of  popular punishment and vengeance in  dealing with those kinds of problems so  I mean how would you go about changing  that it seems like it's part of the  culture at this point having having gone  on for so many years popular punishment  how do you turn that around and how do  you fix something like that well the  interesting thing about culture is that  you can have long established patterns  and there there is always the  possibility of change and change is  continual as well and I think that you  know for if there if if there were some  kind of a  a really widespread localized sort of  forum for talking about the sources of  insecurity and trying to deal with the  sources of insecurity that that central  Africans feel in some kind of effective  way then that might be a way to diminish  the importance of a popular punishment  you know doing that in concert with  trying to support the state security  sector in in ways that will help them to  be more accountable to people and and  more effective in dealing with and the  threats that people feel like they are  facing which primarily have to do with  theft and witchcraft really yeah I was  going to say is you know food an issue  or you know lack of jobs that kind of  thing but that's absolutely an issue was  about but it all happens in a context in  which you know the forces of the occults  the kind of invisible world are seen as  playing a really important role in  people's lives and a really dangerous  role in people's lives that is  fascinating I had no idea that that was  an element so tell us a little bit more  about that historically has that been  the case there yes that has been it has  historically been the case there I mean  it's always talking about witchcraft in  Africa is a really fraud thing to do  because when we hear the word witchcraft  we think you know women wearing black  pointy hats and Halloween and all of  that kind of stuff the Western group we  assume that that's what what we're  dealing with in Africa is well and  instead it's more it more has to do with  the fact that people understand there to  be a visible world that we can see and  observe and then also an invisible world  where there are forces that we can't  control and that those invisible world  forces are also having a really  important impact on the ways that are  the sort of vicissitudes of our lives  and are there some people who are said  to have some control over that other  world yes there are people were referred  to in Ganga the traditional healers  who have a lot of knowledge of the  invisible world and some power to manage  it but one of the reasons why people  feel like there's such pervasive and  security now is that the forms of  witchcraft these kind of occult forces  that are part of people's lives have  really transformed and become ever more  powerful surpassing these in ganga's  capacity to deal with it Wow very  interesting well I could talk to you all  day about that but final question what  do you think the future holds for  central Africa that's a really difficult  question you know the Central African  Republic has been dealt a really  difficult hand by history and  unfortunately that's not going to change  anytime soon I do think that the kind of  war type violence that we've seen over  the past couple of years that is abating  and there have been you know there are a  number of different initiatives that are  being spearheaded by international  actors to try to build peace in the  country you know I have some skepticism  about the approach of a lot of them but  at this point the country has come to  quite a nadir and I I hope that that  means that the only way to go is up even  if that is a very slow and fitful  process right well hopefully that will  be the case thank you so much for being  here was my pleasure and enjoy chatting  your work sure for more information  about professor Lombard and her work  please visit our website at yale edu  slash macmillan report be sure to join  us again for another episode of the  macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale  you

